    "notes": "Without the reference operations the change set would be refused with `broken-reference` naming both referrers, because references are validated against the staged result. The identity `name` is fixed at `create` and immutable afterwards, so there is no shorter way to say this — and that is the point: the delta the user approves is exactly the set of elements the rename edits. Note the two shapes a reference update takes: the connector's endpoint is an object field, so retargeting it is an ordinary `update` at `{field: \"from\"}, {field: \"swc\"}`, while the root's member is identified by its own `name`, so retargeting it is a `remove` plus a `create` at the member addresses. No requirement moves: since contract `11.0.0` the trace link is the component's own `satisfies`, carried inside its `create` payload. The change set is legal precisely because the two SWC identities DIFFER: the protocol bans same-identity replacement globally, for every uniquely identified element and member, so a `remove` and a `create` at the SAME identity is refused as `identity-conflict` (`negative/identity-conflict-same-identity-group-move.json`) — a group move is one instance of that ban, and renaming in place is another. The member pair above is outside it for the same reason the two SWC identities are: its two member names differ."
